### What the rules actually do, measured with two keys that differ in EXACTLY ONE THING: whether the
### caller holds `admin`. Same scopes, same tenant, same everything else — so anything the member
### cannot do is the descriptor and can be nothing else.
###
### The rules under test:
###   people.create      'admin' in @user.roles
###   people.update      'admin' in @user.roles || user_id == @user.id
###   people.list/get    'authenticated' in @user.roles
###   tasks.create       'authenticated' in @user.roles
###   tasks.delete       'admin' in @user.roles
###   milestones.create  'admin' in @user.roles
###
### The headline finding is at the bottom: `'admin' in @user.roles` on CREATE answers 403, and the
### same text on DELETE answers 404. Same rule, two statuses.

### A profile linked to the member's identity — `user_id` is what the update rule compares with
### @user.id, so this row is the one the member owns.
# @name AdminCreatesMemberProfile
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [201]
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/people
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyAdmin}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "{{runToken}}-auth Member Profile",
  "email": "member-{{runToken}}@example.test",
  "user_id": "{{userMember}}"
}

### And somebody else's, linked to no identity at all. The member owns nothing here.
# @name AdminCreatesOtherProfile
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [201]
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/people
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyAdmin}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "{{runToken}}-auth Other Profile",
  "email": "other-{{runToken}}@example.test"
}

### The directory is open to any authenticated caller, so the member sees BOTH — including the row it
### cannot edit. Read access and write access are separate questions, and this is the proof.
# @name MemberReadsDirectory
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/people?name=like.{{runToken}}-auth%25&order=name.asc&limit=100
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyMember}}

### `people.create` names `admin` and the member is not one. A create has no existing row to filter,
### so the rule is evaluated against the CANDIDATE row and rejects it outright — 403.
# @name MemberCannotCreatePerson
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [403]
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/people
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyMember}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "{{runToken}}-auth Smuggled In" }

### The other half of the `||`: the member is not an admin, but `user_id == @user.id` holds on this row.
# @name MemberEditsOwnProfile
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/api/people/{{AdminCreatesMemberProfile.response.body.$.id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyMember}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "{{runToken}}-auth Member Renamed Themselves" }

### The same request against a row the member does not own. Not 403 — **404**. A row-level exclusion is
### deliberately indistinguishable from a row that is not there, because telling them apart is an
### existence oracle. The member can SEE this row in the directory above and still cannot write it.
# @name MemberCannotEditAnother
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [404]
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/api/people/{{AdminCreatesOtherProfile.response.body.$.id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyMember}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "{{runToken}}-auth Hijacked" }

### The control that makes the 404 above a statement about the CALLER: the same row, the same field,
### the admin key — 200. Without this, "that row does not exist" would explain the 404 just as well.
# @name AdminEditsAnotherProfile
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/api/people/{{AdminCreatesOtherProfile.response.body.$.id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyAdmin}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "{{runToken}}-auth Other Profile Edited By Admin" }

### Creating work is open to anyone authenticated — a PM tool where only admins may file a task is
### not a PM tool.
# @name MemberCreatesTask
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [201]
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/tasks
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyMember}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "title": "{{runToken}} m filed by a member", "status": "todo" }

### And here is the finding. `tasks.delete` is `'admin' in @user.roles` — the SAME text as
### `people.create` above, which answered 403. This answers **404**.
###
### Because a delete starts by LOOKING THE ROW UP under the rule as a predicate. For a non-admin the
### predicate is constant-false, so no row matches, so there is nothing to delete — 404. A create has
### no lookup, so its refusal is the only kind left: 403.
# @name MemberCannotDeleteTask
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [404]
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/tasks/{{MemberCreatesTask.response.body.$.id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyMember}}

### The control again: the row is there, and the admin removes it.
# @name AdminDeletesTask
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [204]
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/tasks/{{MemberCreatesTask.response.body.$.id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyAdmin}}

### One more create-shaped refusal, on a different entity, so "403 on create" is the rule's shape and
### not something about `people`.
# @name MemberCannotCreateMilestone
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [403]
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/milestones
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyMember}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "{{runToken}}-auth Member's Plan", "status": "planned" }

### Where the directory ended up
# @name DirectoryAfter
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/people?name=like.{{runToken}}-auth%25&order=name.asc&limit=100
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyAdmin}}
